   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to allowing pre-registered\nvoters to apply for an absentee ballot",
    "basePrintNo": "S6735",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6735-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §8-400, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6735\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to allowing pre-registered\nvoters to apply for an absentee ballot\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 8-400 of the election law to allow a pre-regis-\ntered voter who will be qualified on the date of the next election to\nrequest an absentee ballot\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law and regulations prevent seventeen-year-olds who vote in New\nYork and turn 18 in November before an election from obtaining an absen-\ntee ballot online or by mail. The deadline to request an absentee ballot\nonline or by mail is two weeks prior to the election; requesting an\nabsentee ballot in-person is allowed up to one day before the election.\nThe status quo presents challenges for seventeen-year-old college\nstudents who will be 18 by election day, study in another state, but\nwants to vote absentee and cannot appear in person to request an absen-\ntee ballot.\n\nThis legislation would enable the New York voters who find themselves\ndisenfranchised due to their age and the deadline to request an absentee\nballot the opportunity to vote.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately after becoming law\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
